2026-08-14T01:38:35Z data: {"session_id": "api_1786671305_1e2421e8", "message_id": "msg_546938b46f7f4c23adb873e3541c9088", "content": "I've got to stop and report a real problem rather than give you a fake output — I can't actually run `echo hello`, or any command, right now.\n\nHere's what's happening. The terminal tool is blocked before a single command executes. When it tries to spin up its Docker sandbox, it fails with:\n\n```\nRuntimeError: proxy.enabled is true but iron-proxy is not configured.\nRun `hermes egress setup` to mint tokens and write proxy.yaml.\n```\n\nSo this is a Hermes configuration issue, not something about your command. Your config has outbound-network proxying (`proxy.enabled: true`) turned on, but the iron-proxy config file (`proxy.yaml`) with its minted tokens is missing or not set up. Our memory notes say egress was working before (we added several `extra_allowed_hosts` entries), so it seems something reset or cleared the proxy configuration since then — possibly a config change or a recreated container.\n\nThe fix needs to run on your host machine (where Hermes is installed), not from inside this blocked sandbox. Run:\n\nhermes egress setup\n\nThat mints new tokens and writes proxy.yaml. Then restart the gateway/CLI for it to pick up. If that command isn't available or errors, check the state with:\n\nhermes doctor\n\nand make sure the proxy section didn't get partially cleared.\n\nOnce you've run hermes egress setup (and restarted the session), tell me and I'll rerun echo hello — it should return \"hello\" immediately, since that command doesn't even need network egress.\n\nDo you want me to try anything else meanwhile, or will you run the egress setup on your side first?", "completed": true, "partial": false, "interrupted": false, "runtime": {"provider": "openrouter", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731", "route_source": "global", "requested": {"provider": "", "model": ""}}, "run_id": "run_1fc9919aaced408a97bcb92f1e520b87", "seq": 151, "ts": 1786671515.6185112}
